@anon.9efbc0c128adab6190fabe7f7e045142.1004 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [377 x i8] }> <{ [377 x i8] c"The variants of the symbol.\0A\0ACan be a just a string consisting of a single character for the\0Amodifierless variant or an array with two strings specifying the modifiers\0Aand the symbol. Individual modifiers should be separated by dots. When\0Adisplaying a symbol, Typst selects the first from the variants that have\0Aall attached modifiers and the minimum number of other modifiers." }>, align 1
@anon.9efbc0c128adab6190fabe7f7e045142.1005 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [841 x i8] }> <{ [841 x i8] c"A Unicode symbol.\0A\0ATypst defines common symbols so that they can easily be written with\0Astandard keyboards. The symbols are defined in modules, from which they can\0Abe accessed using [field access notation]($scripting/#fields):\0A\0A- General symbols are defined in the [`sym` module]($category/symbols/sym)\0A- Emoji are defined in the [`emoji` module]($category/symbols/emoji)\0A\0AMoreover, you can define custom symbols with this type's constructor\0Afunction.\0A\0A```example\0A#sym.arrow.r \\\0A#sym.gt.eq.not \\\0A$gt.eq.not$ \\\0A#emoji.face.halo\0A```\0A\0AMany symbols have different variants, which can be selected by appending the\0Amodifiers with dot notation. The order of the modifiers is not relevant.\0AVisit the documentation pages of the symbol modules and click on a symbol to\0Asee its available variants.\0A\0A```example\0A$arrow.l$ \\\0A$arrow.r$ \\\0A$arrow.t.quad$\0A```" }>, align 1
